{"title":"1998 CEMC Gauss (Grade 8) Problems/Problem 21","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1998_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_8)_Problems/Problem_21&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>The number <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/d/6/6d60857d2d582089e51f413f701e45aec6ad1bda.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$315$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"26\" height=\"13\" /> can be written as the product of two odd integers each greater than <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />. In how many ways can this be done?\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/e/7/9e71680e8cea98eb1b0771d6df8166c4ed985c74.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\text{(A)} 0 \\quad\\text{(B)} 1\\quad \\text{(C)} 3\\quad \\text{(D)} 4 \\quad \\text{(E)} 5 \\quad$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"255\" height=\"18\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_1\">Solution 1</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1998_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_8)_Problems/Problem_21&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 1\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>First, we can find the prime factorization of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/d/6/6d60857d2d582089e51f413f701e45aec6ad1bda.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$315$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"26\" height=\"13\" />.\n</p><p>We can see that the number is divisible by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/9/0/79069377f91364c2f87a64e5f9f562a091c8a6c1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> because the last digit is either <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/9/0/79069377f91364c2f87a64e5f9f562a091c8a6c1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> or <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/c/1/bc1f9d9bf8a1b606a4188b5ce9a2af1809e27a89.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />, so we first divide by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/9/0/79069377f91364c2f87a64e5f9f562a091c8a6c1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> to get <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/c/f/4cff5d25891e07b05f6f15796b0ebd09225c1562.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$315 \\div 5 = 63$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"103\" height=\"15\" />. We know that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/4/8/848ec5b5f695afbacb864efead154761481780c0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$63$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" /> is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/6/2/56228dd1dc782a96986b12a6d9070892f64300f8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$9 \\times 7$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"42\" height=\"15\" />, so we have:\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/f/2/5f2cf7f16f67bea730ee293fa13d65bf0cb8ac8c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$315 = 5 \\times 63 = 5 \\times 9 \\times 7 = 3^{2} \\times 5 \\times 7$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"302\" height=\"18\" />\n</p><p>We can now list all of the factor pairs, ignoring those with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> or where the factors are just re-ordered. We then have:\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/5/6/556ae0dabf5b2a4e202d9f312d933c2cbfcab9f6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(3, 15 \\times 7)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"83\" height=\"20\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/a/d/6ad29a066d496d4759fe41dce0d0b360832a3669.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(5, 3^{2} \\times 7)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"81\" height=\"21\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/3/c/33cf5e97a816b8da0a3499c1d1ca63c032e55818.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(7, 3^{2} \\times 5)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"81\" height=\"21\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/a/9/0a9e7acb623d617333179f9da1a89d9ea70d8108.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(3^{2}, 5 \\times 7)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"81\" height=\"21\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/2/f/32f342493c5d17c0095d06f7556fcefc99ecab0a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(3 \\times 5, 3 \\times 7)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"105\" height=\"20\" />\n</p><p>All of the other possible pairs are just reordered so they don't count, and we see that we have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/a/9/ca927d4ce3746ed853fd10f1615d9caa11f6240e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed {\\textbf {(E) } 5}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -11px\" width=\"56\" height=\"30\" />.\n</p><p>~anabel.disher\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_1.5\">Solution 1.5</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1998_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_8)_Problems/Problem_21&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 1.5\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>We can find out that the prime factorization of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/d/6/6d60857d2d582089e51f413f701e45aec6ad1bda.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$315$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"26\" height=\"13\" /> is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/1/3/81314825948c8422ca4b3d78712cd83e7870bef1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3^{2} \\times 5 \\times 7$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"81\" height=\"18\" />, like in solution 1. However, we can also use the fact that the number of factors from its prime factorization is just adding <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> to all of the factors and multiplying the results together. For example, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/b/b/9bb45e7c9d1b06ab69cd8e368fdcb7b1bcad8424.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$15 = 3 \\times 5 = 3^{1} \\times 5^{1}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"164\" height=\"18\" /> has <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/c/c7cab1a05e1e0c1d51a6a219d96577a16b7abf9d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> factors, which can be found by doing <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/5/3/c5360191a32817cce430c4c260e38f20b2bb48b3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(1 + 1) \\times (1 + 1) = 4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"166\" height=\"20\" />.\n</p><p>Applying this to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/d/6/6d60857d2d582089e51f413f701e45aec6ad1bda.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$315$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"26\" height=\"13\" />, we have:\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/7/f/77ff351d666dd7fc1a7022452a18fff9169d4082.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(2 + 1)(1 + 1)(1 + 1) = 3 \\times 2 \\times 2 = 12$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"304\" height=\"20\" /> factors\n</p><p>However, we need to divide by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> and because <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/d/6/6d60857d2d582089e51f413f701e45aec6ad1bda.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$315$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"26\" height=\"13\" /> is not a perfect square so all of its factors come in pairs, so we can just reorder the numbers around. We then need to subtract <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> since <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> isn't allowed as one of the factors in the problem.\n</p><p>This gives <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/9/c/29c87715482ac0b0c8a371a721251de7c39d2270.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$12 \\div 2 - 1 = \\boxed {\\textbf {(E) } 5}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -11px\" width=\"161\" height=\"30\" />.\n</p><p>~anabel.disher\n</p>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black; width:80%;\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"background:#ccf;text-align:center;\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1998_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_8)\" title=\"1998 CEMC Gauss (Grade 8)\">1998 CEMC Gauss (Grade 8)</a></b> (<b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1998_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_8)_Problems&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"1998 CEMC Gauss (Grade 8) Problems (page does not exist)\">Problems</a></b> • <b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1998_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_8)_Answer_Key&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"1998 CEMC Gauss (Grade 8) Answer Key (page does not exist)\">Answer Key</a></b> • <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://cemc.math.uwaterloo.ca/contests/past_contests.html\">Resources</a>)\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Preceded&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1998_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_8)_Problems/Problem_20\" title=\"1998 CEMC Gauss (Grade 8) Problems/Problem 20\">Problem 20</a></b>\n</td>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Followed&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1998_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_8)_Problems/Problem_22\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"1998 CEMC Gauss (Grade 8) Problems/Problem 22\">Problem 22</a></b>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"3\" style=\"text-align:center;\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1998_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_8)_Problems/Problem_1\" title=\"1998 CEMC Gauss (Grade 8) Problems/Problem 1\">1</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1998_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_8)_Problems/Problem_2\" title=\"1998 CEMC Gauss (Grade 8) Problems/Problem 2\">2</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1998_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_8)_Problems/Problem_3\" title=\"1998 CEMC Gauss (Grade 8) Problems/Problem 3\">3</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1998_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_8)_Problems/Problem_4&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"1998 CEMC Gauss (Grade 8) Problems/Problem 4 (page does not exist)\">4</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1998_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_8)_Problems/Problem_5\" title=\"1998 CEMC Gauss (Grade 8) Problems/Problem 5\">5</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1998_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_8)_Problems/Problem_6\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"1998 CEMC Gauss (Grade 8) Problems/Problem 6\">6</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1998_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_8)_Problems/Problem_7\" title=\"1998 CEMC Gauss (Grade 8) Problems/Problem 7\">7</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1998_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_8)_Problems/Problem_8\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"1998 CEMC Gauss (Grade 8) Problems/Problem 8\">8</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1998_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_8)_Problems/Problem_9\" title=\"1998 CEMC Gauss (Grade 8) Problems/Problem 9\">9</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1998_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_8)_Problems/Problem_10\" title=\"1998 CEMC Gauss (Grade 8) Problems/Problem 10\">10</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1998_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_